{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___. I'm Dr. ___ from the ICU. You were brought in after some falls and very low blood pressure. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’ve been feeling more tired and weak over the past week. My blood pressure has been okay at home, around 120 to 130 systolic. But two nights ago, it dropped to 100, and then kept going down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Did you take your usual medications that day?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I took my carvedilol and torsemide. I didn’t think it was a problem at the time. But then I got dizzy, vomited once, and fell three times. The last fall scared me, so I called EMS."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. Your blood pressure was 70 over 33 when you arrived at the outside hospital. You were given fluids and a medication called levophed to support your blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that why I’m on a drip now? I feel a bit better, but still weak."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:26:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, you still need some support. We placed a central line in your neck to give you fluids and medications more effectively. Your kidneys were stressed—your creatinine was high at 2.8—but it’s already improving."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that’s good. I’ve had kidney issues before. Is this the same thing?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It looks like pre-renal failure—meaning your kidneys didn’t get enough blood flow, likely from low blood pressure and volume. Now that we’re supporting you, your creatinine is dropping to 1.6 and should return to your baseline."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. I also hit my head during the falls. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a head CT and a neck CT. No fractures or bleeding. You’re safe from serious injury, but we’ll keep an eye on your headache."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks. I’ve been on prednisone for my arthritis. I recently reduced it from 8 to 7 mg. Could that be part of the problem?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Excellent question. That could definitely play a role. Your body may have become dependent on steroids, and even a small drop can cause adrenal insufficiency—your body can’t respond to stress like low blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I need more steroids now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. You received a high dose of hydrocortisone in the ER. We’re now transitioning you to 10 mg of oral prednisone daily. Endocrine agrees this is appropriate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And what about my other meds? I take a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We held your carvedilol and torsemide temporarily because they lower blood pressure. Once you’re stable, we’ll restart them at lower doses."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Makes sense. I’ve also had some diarrhea the past two nights."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked for C. diff—your test came back negative. It’s likely an IBS flare. We’ve given Donnatal to help with the cramps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2176-06-19 14:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to know. I was worried it was an infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your blood pressure is much better—118 over 76 this morning. We’ve turned off the levophed. How are you feeling?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Much better. Not dizzy anymore. Can I sit up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ll help you sit and even walk later. Your labs are improving—creatinine is now 1.2, and your blood sugar is stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What about my insulin? I usually count carbs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We consulted diabetes. You’re now on 20 units of detemir in the morning and 29 at night, plus aspart for meals. You can resume carb counting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Perfect. And my blood pressure meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re restarting carvedilol at 6.25 mg twice daily—half your usual dose. Torsemide will start at 50 mg daily due to your leg swelling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on 100 mg. Is 50 enough?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re starting low to avoid another drop in blood pressure. If swelling worsens, you can go back to 100 mg. We’re also stopping spironolactone for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. What about my arthritis meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Azathioprine was restarted yesterday. Ustekinumab will resume after discharge. Rheumatology approved this plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my neuropathy meds—nortriptyline, pregabalin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2176-06-20 08:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They were restarted last night at your usual doses. You should feel the benefit soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2176-06-21 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs continue to improve. Creatinine is 1.0, and your CBC is stable. We’re moving you to the regular floor today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2176-06-21 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. I feel ready. Any more tests?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2176-06-21 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a chest X-ray to check your central line. It’s in good position. No pneumonia or fluid in the lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2176-06-21 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had a headache. Is that from the fall?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2176-06-21 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely from the trauma, but your CT was clear. We’ll keep giving acetaminophen. Let us know if it worsens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing really well. BP is 128 over 74, no chest pain, and your EKG shows stable prolonged QT—no changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been monitoring at home. I’ll keep doing that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, please do. We’re also sending you home with a rescue dose of dexamethasone—in case your blood pressure drops again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "An injection? How do I use it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a single dose—4 mg. You inject it into your thigh muscle if your systolic drops below 90 and you feel dizzy or weak. Then call your doctor or go to the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. What else should I watch for?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Weigh yourself daily. If you gain more than 2 pounds in a day or notice increased leg swelling, adjust your torsemide as we discussed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my prednisone? I was worried about reducing it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2176-06-22 10:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll stay on 10 mg daily for now. Don’t stop it suddenly. Your endocrinologist will help you taper it safely later."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2176-06-23 11:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re cleared for discharge. Your blood pressure is stable, labs are good, and you’re walking independently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2176-06-23 11:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I feel much better. I’ll follow up with my PCP and cardiologist."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2176-06-23 11:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and with rheumatology and endocrinology. Keep using your CPAP and take your meds as prescribed. Call us if anything changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2176-06-23 11:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for taking such good care of me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2176-06-23 11:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2176-06-19 14:13:00",
    "admission_end": "2176-06-23 19:23:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old male with a history of psoriatic arthritis, steroid dependence, and multiple comorbidities was admitted for hypotension and recurrent falls following a reduction in prednisone dose. He presented with systolic blood pressure as low as 70, requiring vasopressor support and ICU admission. Evaluation revealed acute kidney injury likely due to hypovolemia, negative head and neck imaging after trauma from falls, and no evidence of sepsis or acute cardiac event. Adrenal insufficiency was considered a contributing factor. He was treated with stress-dose hydrocortisone, fluid resuscitation, and vasopressors, which were successfully weaned. His blood pressure stabilized, renal function improved, and he was transitioned to 10 mg daily oral prednisone with a rescue dexamethasone dose for home use. Other medications were adjusted, including reduction of carvedilol and temporary hold of spironolactone, with torsemide restarted at 50 mg daily. He developed a diarrhea episode consistent with IBS, which resolved with supportive care. He was discharged home in stable condition with follow-up plans across multiple specialties.",
